guides=[('guest-positioning-guide','Position your rental around the right guest','A practical starting point for identifying who your property serves best.', [('Start with the travel need','Describe why someone travels, who travels with them, and what a successful stay looks like. A purpose such as a quiet anniversary break is more useful than an age group alone.'),('Check the evidence','Look for repeated experiences in guest reviews and recurring questions in enquiries. Separate consistent strengths from occasional perks, and record where the property is a poor fit.'),('Choose the strongest promise','Connect a guest priority to something your property can reliably deliver. Make the promise specific and support it with accurate photos, amenities and location detail.'),('Apply it across the listing','Use the title and first photos to establish the experience. Expand the reasons to choose the stay in your description and answer practical concerns before they interrupt a booking.'),('Review by season','Different weeks can attract different travel needs. Revisit the guest profile when the reason to travel changes, while keeping the property facts consistent.')]),('weekly-revenue-guide','Connect your weekly pricing and marketing','A simple planning routine for keeping demand, price and promotion aligned.', [('Review the booking position','Compare booked nights, lead time and pace for the weeks ahead. Separate owner-blocked nights from nights you genuinely intend to sell.'),('Identify the reason to travel','Consider the relevant guest, seasonal activities, events and booking window for each period. Verify event dates before building a campaign around them.'),('Choose the commercial response','Decide whether the gap calls for a pricing adjustment, a stay-rule change, clearer positioning or more relevant promotion. Account for the cost of acquiring the booking.'),('Coordinate the message','Match the offer and marketing theme to the target guest. Check that the message, availability and booking rules agree across channels.'),('Measure what happened','Review booking pickup and revenue alongside campaign activity. Treat observed changes as signals to investigate, rather than proof that one action caused the result.')]),('automation-guide','Choose your first automation with confidence','Start with a repetitive workflow that has a clear trigger and an observable outcome.', [('Map the current task','Write down what starts the task, the information it needs, the steps involved and who is responsible. Identify exceptions before choosing a tool.'),('Use what you already have','Check your PMS, CRM and existing integrations for relevant features. Avoid adding a subscription that duplicates something your current system can do.'),('Define the boundaries','Agree what the automation can change, when it must pause, and how it should escalate an exception. Keep human review where a mistake would materially affect a guest or booking.'),('Test a complete journey','Use test records to check triggers, timing, duplicate messages and failure handling. Confirm that the right person can see when a workflow fails.'),('Review the result','Compare the manual effort removed with the effort required to monitor and maintain the workflow. Keep the automation only if it improves the overall process.')])]
